@* Components/AppAlert.razor — shared inline alert/banner component
   (CLAUDE.md §3.1). Covers Screen B's "إجراء مطلوب" warning strip and the
   "اخترت عدة أسئلة..." success strip (both in
   demo/ScreenB/TaskTwo_from_figma_design.png) as ONE component with a
   Severity param, not two — both strips share identical anatomy (icon +
   message, tinted bg/icon/text per severity). See tokens.css's own "Alert"
   section for the full color/contrast writeup and DECISIONS.md for the
   accessible-substitute candidate table.

   ANATOMY, pixel-confirmed against the reference PNG (CLAUDE.md rule 1),
   not assumed: a content-sized, fully-rounded pill — NOT full-width, NOT
   bordered (a direct tint-to-page-background transition was found at every
   edge of both pills, no separate border-stroke hue in between; corrects
   the original "background tint + border" anatomy assumption). Icon
   leading — renders at the RTL right/start edge, same "first DOM child =
   RTL right edge" convention already established by AppActionBanner/
   AppStatusCard's own icon badges.

   FLAGGED, not silently normalized: the warning pill's icon sits measurably
   closer to mid-pill in the raw reference than flush against its right
   edge, unlike the success pill (icon flush against its own right edge).
   Most likely a bidi-rendering artifact of an icon glyph sitting inline
   inside a mixed-direction Arabic string in the source design tool's
   export, not a deliberate "icon between two text runs" layout — both
   severities are standardized here on the same icon-leading/right-edge
   layout for internal consistency and to match the rest of this codebase.
   Flag if the offset position in the warning reference was actually
   intentional.

   ICONS — confirmed two ways (CLAUDE.md rule 1): (1) grepped the compiled,
   installed MudBlazor.dll's own strings for the exact constant names,
   not assumed from memory — both exist in this project's MudBlazor 8.7;
   (2) zoomed pixel crops of the reference PNG confirmed the actual glyph
   shapes. Warning is an OUTLINED/rounded triangle with "!"
   (Icons.Material.Filled.WarningAmber) — NOT the solid-filled
   Icons.Material.Filled.Warning. Success is an OUTLINED circle with a
   checkmark (Icons.Material.Filled.CheckCircleOutline) — NOT the solid
   Icons.Material.Filled.CheckCircle.

   role="alert" (Warning) vs role="status" (Success) — CLAUDE.md §6. Warning
   is the "you cannot proceed until this is fixed" case, matching
   role="alert"'s implicit aria-live="assertive"; Success is an advisory
   confirmation of a completed user action, matching role="status"'s
   implicit aria-live="polite" — the same urgent/advisory split ARIA's own
   authoring practices document for these two roles.

   Icon is wrapped in a plain aria-hidden="true" span (not an attribute on
   MudIcon itself) — same established pattern as AppActionBanner's badge and
   AppStatusCard's icon badge, so the message text (real, visible,
   already-confirmed-contrast text) is the only thing announced, not the
   decorative icon.

   No focus ring / touch-target sizing here (see components.css's own note
   on this component): it renders no button/link, only a status pill, so
   neither CLAUDE.md §6 rule applies. *@

<div class="app-alert app-alert--@SeverityClass" role="@RoleForSeverity">
    <span class="app-alert__icon" aria-hidden="true">
        <MudIcon Icon="@IconForSeverity" Color="Color.Inherit" Size="Size.Small" />
    </span>
    <span class="app-alert__message">@Message</span>
</div>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public AppAlertSeverity Severity { get; set; }

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Message { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    /// Override the per-severity default icon. Most consumers should leave
    /// this unset — the default icons are already confirmed against the
    /// Figma reference (see header comment above).
    [Parameter] public string? Icon { get; set; }

    private string SeverityClass => Severity switch
    {
        AppAlertSeverity.Success => "success",
        _ => "warning",
    };

    private string IconForSeverity => Icon ?? Severity switch
    {
        AppAlertSeverity.Success => Icons.Material.Filled.CheckCircleOutline,
        _ => Icons.Material.Filled.WarningAmber,
    };

    private string RoleForSeverity => Severity switch
    {
        AppAlertSeverity.Success => "status",
        _ => "alert",
    };
}
